codex
The captured failure is precise: direct base CPython on Windows always includes its installation root in `sys.path`, alongside `Lib` and `DLLs`. Treating only the two subdirectories as the runtime identity therefore rejects a legitimate path—and permitting the root without binding it would be unsafe. I’m tightening the model so the Windows runtime identity fingerprints the entire importable Python home (while still excluding disabled site-package trees), then the worker may accept that root because every loadable file beneath it is covered by pre-launch and post-exec verification.
